    "created_at": "2016-05-23T11:41:12Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-05-23T11:50:56Z",
    "body": "@domenic `rel=next` already had use cases prior to this change. One being search engine semantics, and the other being accessibility tools.\n\nFor example, I have a \"next page\" and \"previous page\" keyboard binds that run some JS that checks for rel=next/prev, and if none are found it falls back on incrementing/decrementing the first contiguous integer found in the URL. As a result of this change, websites that don't get much next page (the majority) traffic will now need to **break accessibility** to prevent their users from preloading the next page.\n\nI don't use any screen readers, but I would assume that similar functionality is included in many of them for general \"go to next page\" logic.\n\nSites that get a lot of next page traffic (I would imagine that this is the minority) can simply add `prefetch` or `preload` to their next page link's rel list.\n\nThe use case described was already solved with `rel=preload` and `rel=prerender`. This change hurts the majority of websites for the benefit of a very slim minority of websites. Google search would be hurt by this change if it used this rel value semantically in its next page links, as they have stated that the overwhelming majority of users get what they need from page 1.\n\nIf this change is going to stay, we need a new rel value for `next` that doesn't involve preloading.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
