    "created_at": "2016-05-23T13:04:48Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-05-23T13:07:43Z",
    "body": "The idea of browsers preloading pages linked to with `rel=next` has actually been in the official HTML specification since version 3.2 (https://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html32#link). But there is a difference: Back then it had been a “can be used to”, now it is “user agents should”.\n\n@domenic \n\n> It's important to spec the world as it is, not the world as we wish it would be.\n\nNothing forces you to turn a “can” into a “should” or “must”, though.\n\n(On a side note, I’m annoyed that search engines suddenly decided to take `rel=next`/`prev` as a strong hint to prefer the first page in the series in search results. That has suddenly changed the semantics of `rel=next`/`prev` without necessity [as they could have introduced new `rel` values for this purpose] and apparently without discussion in the community. It also means that a lot of legacy content is interpreted differently from what their authors had intended.)\n",
